WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:11.300
car chases make up some of the most
thrilling scenes in movie history. We've

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:22.120
seen them in some form or another since
the early days of moviemaking

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.200
We talked to Wade Eastwood, a stunt
coordinator who most recently worked on

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.520
"Mission Impossible: Fallout." One of the
movies best scenes was a car chase

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.290
through Paris. We asked him: how do you
train to be in a car chase,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:47.680
how do you plan one, and when the day
comes how do you actually shoot it?

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.920
Here's a little peek into how an epic
car chase is brought to life.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:07.180
Before a shoot stunt drivers will drive
around in a variety of different

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:13.240
vehicles to get the feel of how each of
them drives. Training most often focuses

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.250
on improving drift and precision skills.
Actors and stunt drivers will drive

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:28.530
around surrounded by cones to keep them on mark. Being a stunt coordinator

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:34.720
doesn't just mean planning action scenes.
Productions sometimes pour millions of

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.270
dollars into a given car chase so it's
necessary that it is done as safely as

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.840
possible.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.940
[Wade] Before I start testing I have
to look at what we want to do and see

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.920
how much risk I can eliminate and then
during the testing phase we evaluate

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.100
my risk assessment and when I get to a
place that my risk assessment is

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:01.460
not a high risk assessment it's a medium
to low risk then it's a doable stunt.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.400
They have to assess the surface they're
going to be shooting on.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.360
[Wade] Terrain

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:11.050
plays an important role when building a
car chase. The cobbled streets in Paris,

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.000
obviously slippery surfaces. Cobblestones

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.030
can move you've got you know then

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:21.040
you keep transitioning from cobbles to
tarmac, asphalt, so you've got different

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.450
grip levels and you might be on a
mountain road that's dusty because the

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:29.290
winds have blown. When I'm doing a
sequence I'll always try and sweep the

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:33.459
roads and clean them so there's
consistency to the surface. [Narrator] Once the casting

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.040
crew is thoroughly prepared it's time to
film the chase

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:46.000
To capture the most dynamic chase scene
it's important to shoot it from every

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:52.680
possible angle. It's not unusual to see a
car covered in cameras. Camera mounts are

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:57.220
attached to a car as well as lights.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.700
The cars in Fallout were free-driven. In some

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:06.480
instances though the car being filmed
will be towed. This is usually done when

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.829
there's a lot of dialogue and a need for
the driver to concentrate on their

00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:16.769
performance. It's also best to do this if
the performer's vision is too obscured by

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:22.200
all the cameras mounted to the hood of
the car. To get every angle imaginable a

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.019
crew will use a 'Russian arm' camera
tracking system. In addition Eastwood

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:33.239
swears by a tracking motorcycle that he
uses. He mounts a stabilized camera to a

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:38.159
bike which can go from one inch off the
ground to seven feet off the ground in

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:45.449
just two seconds. A lot of the car chases
you see are done practically. Sure visual

00:03:45.449 --> 00:03:49.620
effects have to be used for certain
details but it's rare you'll find a car

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.209
chase done completely in front of a
green screen. You'll really only see that

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:59.359
for certain stunts that are too
dangerous to do in real life

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.970
Even when a car chase is shot practically
there will always be an element of

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:16.080
visual trickery involved. [Wade] Making fifty
miles an hour

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.350
looked like a hundred miles and look
exciting which is the trick that we have

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.590
to do in film because for everyone to
keep up with you at a hundred miles an

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.850
hour cameras and things happen far too
quickly and accidents would happen.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:31.800
So we have to tone things
down but make the car look exciting.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.960
[Narrator] But making a good car chase is about more
than just producing thrills, driving fast

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.370
and conquering the most impossible of
terrains. [Wade] That's not always the big

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.390
spectacle for me with a car chase, it
has to be story. If I'm invested in the

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:52.170
character in the car, then what happens
with the car is immaterial to me but it's

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.460
more about going on the journey with
that character in the car. [Narrator] It's why

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.350
Tom Cruise, Vin Diesel, and audiences
everywhere will never be able to get

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.490
enough of a good car chase.

